    overview: "Begin with God's name and end with praise. Say the name with each mouthful, growing the phrase by one word each time. Eat with the right hand, start and end with salt, and take small mouthfuls chewed well. Never fault food; the Prophet never did. Eat from the side of the dish near you, except with fruit.",
    thesis: "The second part of the chapter is about the meal itself. Nearly every manner in it slows the eater down or turns him towards God. The name keeps greed from crowding out remembrance. The small, well-chewed morsel resists haste. And the Prophet's silence about food he disliked is the model for the tongue.",
    moves: [
      { title: "The name, and praise", body: "The second part covers the manners of the meal itself. He begins with “In the name of God” and ends with “Praise be to God.” If he says “In the name of God” with every mouthful, that is good, so that greed does not distract him from remembering God." },
      { title: "A growing phrase", body: "With the first mouthful he says “In the name of God.” With the second, “In the name of God, the Merciful.” With the third, “In the name of God, the Merciful, the Compassionate.” He says it aloud, to remind anyone else at the meal." },
      { title: "Right hand and salt", body: "He eats with his right hand. He begins with salt and ends with it." },
      { title: "Small, chewed, one at a time", body: "He makes each mouthful small and chews it well. He does not reach for the next mouthful until he has swallowed the last. Reaching early is haste in eating." },
      { title: "He never faulted food", body: "He does not criticise any food. The messenger of God never found fault with food. If he liked it, he ate it. Otherwise, he left it." },
      { title: "From what is near you", body: "He eats from what is in front of him, except fruit, where he may move his hand around. The Prophet said: “Eat from what is next to you.” Then he himself used to move his hand around a plate of fruit. When asked about it, he said: “It is not all one kind.”" },
      { title: "Not from the top", body: "He does not eat from the top of the heaped dish or the middle of the food. He eats from the edge of the round loaf. If bread is short, he breaks the bread; he does not cut it with a knife. Nor does he cut meat with a knife. The Prophet forbade that and said: “Tear it with your teeth.”" },
    ],
